            "title": "Trade War Update: Renewed Optimism Ahead Of Xi-Trump G20 Meeting",
            "snippets": [
              "China shares rise on hopes yet again that a truce is coming in the trade war.\nMoney",
              "# Trade War Update: Renewed Optimism Ahead Of Xi-Trump G20 Meeting\nCould they? Investors hope the midterms were a signal to Trump to push ahead with a trade deal with... [+] his Chinese counterpart, Xi Jinping. Trump said he is hopeful for a \"great deal\", but added that China was not ready for one.",
              "China's stock markets are looking good this morning. And it's hopes that Xi Jinping and Donald Trump can set the table for some sort of bilateral trade agreement at the G20 meeting taking place in Buenos Aires on Nov.",
              "Backroom talks between the U.S. and China have begun once again in preparation for the two presidents to meet in two weeks. The Chinese stock market has been in a bear market since the first quarter and has never recovered since tariffs were enacted in June and July. China has retaliated, leading many Democrats in farm districts to gain new seats in the House of Representatives as a new anti-tariff voice for farmers",
              "\"The hopes are that both countries would be able to strike a deal and put the woes of trade war behind them,\" says Naeem Aslam, chief market strategist for ThinkMarkets in London.\n\nTrade war woes have created a meaningful impact on global growth, with European and Chinese equity markets taking the biggest hits. The U.S. stock market has outperformed them both, but the S&P 500's gains on the year have been lackluster",
              "In other market crystal balls, the U.S.-China bond yield spread  -- the difference between yield between the 10 year bond of both governments -- is at its tightest level since July 2010",
              "China has emerged as a serious competitor to U.S. high tech companies in Asia. Although China says its new military buildup is not meant to threaten the U.S. in the Pacific Ocean, it is clearly a deterrent to the strongest Navy fleet in the region, which just happens to be American. China is rising, and Washington under Trump wants to put a lid on it. The president has boasted a number of manufacturing jobs being set up in the U.S",
              "The U.S. China Business Council has been lobbying for a bilateral trade agreement for years. President Obama sought to surround China with a free trade agreement of Pacific nations called the Trans-Pacific Partnership, but Trump nixed it practically on day one of his presidency.",
              "Other than a few decent earnings out of Europe, the mood today is expected to be largely positive thanks to word that China’s Vice Premier Liu He is coming to Washington to pave the way for a meeting between Trump and Xi in Argentina, Bloomberg reported this morning.\n\nEditorial Standards Reprints & Permissions",

